.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH PRINTF 3S
.SH NAME
printf, fprintf, sprintf, snprintf, vprintf, vfprintf, vsprintf, vsnprintf \-
formatted output conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int printf(const char *format, ...);
.PP
.B int fprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...);
.PP
.B int sprintf(char *s, const char *format, ...);
.PP
.B int snprintf(char *s, size_t size, const char *format, ...);
.PP
.B #include <stdarg.h>
.PP
.B int vprintf(const char *format, va_list ap);
.PP
.B int vfprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, va_list ap);
.PP
.B int vsprintf(char *s, const char *format, va_list ap);
.PP
.B int vsnprintf(char *s, size_t size, const char *format, va_list ap);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Printf
places output on the standard output stream
.IR stdout .
.I Fprintf
places output on the named output
.IR stream .
.I Sprintf
places `output' in the string
.I s,
followed by the character `\\0'.
.PP
.B Note:
.I snprintf
is C11 and writes at most
.I size
bytes including the terminating null; the four
.I v
forms take an already-started
.B va_list
in place of the variable argument list.  v7 had none of the eight but the first
three.  See BESM-6 NOTES for which of them is the primitive here.
.PP
Each of these functions
converts, formats, and prints its arguments after the first
under control of the first argument.
The first argument is a character string
which contains
two types of objects:
plain characters, which are simply copied to the
output stream,
and conversion specifications,
each of which causes conversion and printing
of the next successive
.I arg
.IR printf .
.PP
Each conversion specification is introduced by
the character
.BR % .
Following the
.BR % ,
there may be
.TP
\-
zero or more flags:
.B \-
for
.I "left adjustment"
of the converted value in the indicated field;
.B 0
for zero-padding instead of blank-padding;
.B +
or a space, to force a sign or a blank in front of a signed conversion; and
.B #
for the alternate form, which prefixes a non-zero
.B o
with `0' and a non-zero
.B x
or
.B X
with `0x' or `0X'.
.B Note:
v7's page described only the first two.
.TP
\-
an optional digit string specifying a
.I "field width;"
if the converted value has fewer characters
than the field width
it will be blank-padded on the left (or right,
if the left-adjustment indicator has been
given) to make up the field width;
.TP
\-
an optional period
.RB ` . '
which serves to
separate the field width from the
next digit string;
.TP
\-
an optional digit string
specifying a
.I precision
which specifies
the number of digits to appear after the
decimal point, for e- and f-conversion,
or the maximum number of characters
to be printed from a string;
.TP
\-
an optional length modifier.
.B Note:
v7 had only
.BR l ,
and said that a capitalized conversion code did the same thing.
.B Neither is true here.
The C11 set
.RB ( hh ", " h ", " l ", " ll ", " j ", " z ", " t ", " L )
is accepted and
.I every
member of it is
.IR ignored ,
because every argument is exactly one word and
.I va_arg
steps by one word whatever type it is named with.  And a capital conversion
letter is a
.I different
conversion now \- see below.
.TP
\-
a character which indicates the type of
conversion to be applied.
.PP
A field width or precision may be `*' instead of a digit string.
In this case an integer
.I arg
supplies
the field width or precision.
.PP
The conversion characters
and their meanings are
.TP
.B diouxX
The integer
.I arg
is converted to decimal
.RB ( d ", " i ),
unsigned decimal
.RB ( u ),
octal
.RB ( o ),
or hexadecimal
.RB ( x ", " X )
notation respectively.
.B Note:
.B i
is C11's synonym for
.BR d .
.TP
.B p
The
.I arg
is printed as an unsigned hexadecimal value in the alternate form.  C11; v7 had
no such conversion.
.TP
.B f
The float or double
.I arg
is converted to decimal notation
in the style `[\fB\-\fR]ddd.ddd'
where the number of d's after the decimal point
is equal to the precision specification
for the argument.
If the precision
is missing,
6 digits are given;
if the precision is explicitly 0, no digits and
no decimal point are printed.
.TP
.B e
The float or double
.I arg
is converted in the style
`[\fB\-\fR]d\fB.\fRddd\fBe\fR\(+-dd'
where there is one digit before the decimal point and
the number after is equal to the
precision specification for the argument;
when the precision is missing,
6 digits are produced.
.TP
.B g
The float or double
.I arg
is printed in style
.BR d ,
in style
.BR f ,
or in
style
.BR e ,
whichever gives full precision in minimum space.
.TP
.B "F E G"
As
.BR f ", " e " and " g ,
but any letter the conversion emits is upper case.
.B Note:
under v7 a capital was merely a length modifier and printed identically; see
BESM-6 NOTES.
.TP
.B c
The character
.I arg
is printed.
.B Note:
v7 said null characters are ignored.  They are not: a `\\0' is emitted like any
other byte, and a stream carries it through.
.TP
.B s
.I Arg
is taken to be a string (character pointer)
and characters from the string are printed until
a null character or until
the number of characters indicated by the precision
specification is reached;
however if the precision is 0 or missing
all characters up to a null are printed.
.B Note:
a null pointer prints as
.RB ` (null) '
rather than faulting.
.TP
.B n
The number of characters produced so far is stored through the
.B int *
.IR arg .
Nothing is printed.  C11; v7 had no such conversion.
.TP
.B %
Print a `%'; no argument is converted.
.PP
.B Note:
an unrecognised conversion letter is echoed verbatim, preceded by its `%'.
.PP
In no case does a non-existent or small field width
cause truncation of a field;
padding takes place only if the specified field
width exceeds the actual width.
Characters generated by
.I printf
are printed by
.IR putc (3).
.PP
.B Examples
.br
To print a date and time in the form `Sunday, July 3, 10:02',
where
.I weekday
and
.I month
are pointers to null-terminated strings:
.RS
.HP
.nh
printf("%s, %s %d, %02d:%02d", weekday, month, day, hour, min);
.RE
.hy
.PP
To print
.if n pi
.if t \(*p
to 5 decimals:
.IP
printf("pi = %.5f", 4*atan(1.0));
.SH "SEE ALSO"
putc(3),
scanf(3),
ecvt(3),
stdio(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Note:
v7's page said nothing about return values, because K&R had not settled them.
Each of the eight returns the number of characters produced, and
.I snprintf
and
.I vsnprintf
return the number that
.I would
have been produced had the buffer been large enough \- which may exceed
.IR size .
.SH BUGS
.B Note:
v7's bug, "very wide fields (>128 characters) fail", is gone: padding is emitted
in a loop and passes through no buffer at all.  Only the
.I digits
of a conversion are buffered, and 32 of them is more than a 48-bit value can
need.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS This engine is not v7's
v7's
.I _doprnt
was x86 assembly in the port these sources came from, so there was nothing to
port.  The engine here is the external c-compiler's
.IR doprnt.c ,
itself derived from the FreeBSD kernel
.IR printf ,
retargeted to a
.B FILE *
sink.
.SS The case fold is gone, which is why a capital is a real conversion
The engine arrived folding
.I every
conversion letter to upper case, because its output device buffered KOI7 and
printed upper case only:
.B %d
and
.B %D
both arrived as `D', and %x/%X, %e/%E and %g/%G were indistinguishable.
.PP
.B This terminal is ASCII,
so the letters are case-sensitive again \- three distinct pairs, plus a
.B "(null)"
that is no longer
.BR (NULL) .
.SS The v form is the primitive
<stdarg.h> is the only way to walk an argument list here, so
.I vfprintf
is the routine and the seven others are wrappers around it.  That is the
inverse of v7, where
.I _doprnt
took a frame pointer and the
.I v
forms did not exist.
.SS sprintf counts because the stream drops the byte
.I Sprintf
and
.I snprintf
build a v7 _IOSTRG stream over the caller's buffer.  When such a stream fills,
.IR _flsbuf ()
.I drops
the byte and leaves the count rising \- which is exactly the value C11 wants
.I snprintf
to return, obtained for nothing.
.SS %d of the most negative value
Taking the magnitude as \-(int)ul is wrong here twice over: a signed value is a
41-bit field with bits 48..42 zero, so negating it as a signed
.B int
overflows at the negative limit, and negating the raw word as unsigned is wrong
for
.I every
negative, the word not being a true 48-bit two's complement.  The magnitude is
computed as 2**41 \- ul in unsigned arithmetic instead.
.SS %010.2f keeps its zero fill
C11 sec 7.21.6.1p6 says a precision makes the `0' flag ignored \- but only for
.BR diouxX .
The engine this came from applied it in the flag parser and silently disarmed
the floating conversions too.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/doprnt.c and the eight entry points beside it
